# Release Safety Gates

- When editing CI, build, deploy, startup, or healthcheck files, optimize for trustworthy release signals, not just green builds.
- CI must run the checks that actually protect the changed area; do not let tests exist only locally.
- Keep workspace and tool versions aligned across repo config, Dockerfiles, and workflows.
- Startup should fail fast when migrations or required readiness steps fail. Do not continue serving in a known-unsafe schema state.
- Healthchecks must prove the level of health they claim. Use shallow liveness and deeper readiness intentionally; do not point container readiness at a route that only proves the process booted.
- For **multiple Cloud Run (or any) instances**, Socket.IO room broadcasts require a shared adapter: set **`SOCKET_IO_REDIS_URL`** (or **`REDIS_URL`**) so the server can attach the Redis adapter at startup; otherwise realtime is single-process only.

```yaml
# ✅ minimum meaningful verification today (repo-wide eslint/prettier are backlog)
- pnpm type-check
- pnpm --filter vssyl-web test   # vitest: web/src/runtime/** only (see web/vitest.config.ts)
- pnpm test                      # server integration/unit (root script)
- pnpm security:secrets          # working-tree Gitleaks (.gitleaks.toml); also .github/workflows/secret-scan.yml
# Optional when green: pnpm lint, package format:check
# Manual only: pnpm security:secrets:history (may flag rotated historical secrets)
```

- Secret scanning is part of repository/release hygiene: run `pnpm security:secrets` (config: `.gitleaks.toml`) for the current tree; CI enforces the same via `.github/workflows/secret-scan.yml`. Do not commit literal credentials in source, scripts, docs, Memory Bank, or archives.
- If deploy automation changes, also verify rollback guidance or operator docs still match the real release path.
